  tenant="Faux Plants and Trees" products=5       t0_rss_kb=2184     with_tenant_rss_kb=2388     peak_serving_rss_kb=2584     peak_growth_kb=196      post_teardown_rss_kb=2712     total_queries_served=82274156  samples: t5s=2584KB t10s=2584KB t15s=2584KB t20s=2584KB
  tenant="Water Filter Pitchers" products=1       t0_rss_kb=2188     with_tenant_rss_kb=2392     peak_serving_rss_kb=2588     peak_growth_kb=196      post_teardown_rss_kb=2716     total_queries_served=195755448  samples: t5s=2584KB t10s=2588KB t15s=2588KB t20s=2588KB

H7 comparison: idle-resident peak growth=244.0 KB (11.2% of t0), max active-resident tenant peak growth=896 KB
  H7 verdict: CONFIRMED -- peak RSS while actually serving is materially higher than H6's immediate snapshot; a genuinely long-running service costs more than the short-lived-process floor
  (note: post-teardown RSS, printed above, is a distinct secondary signal about thread-join/shutdown behavior -- not used for this verdict, since a real long-running service does not tear down its worker pool mid-life)

